From 857f1e158c9f449ee032f227b7f70ac4991aa40b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 01:06:24 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 146/175] feat: the look moves to its own component - CineCam
 Grade (S10 item 2)

The ASC CDL and the LMT slot are no longer part of every cinematic camera.
They move, unchanged, onto a new entity component CCineGradeComponent
(editor label "CineCam Grade", category Cameras, same plugin) that you add
to the same entity as a Cinematic Camera. A look is a thing you add, copy
to another camera and delete - the same story design decision D10 tells
about the LUT file itself - and a camera that never grades no longer pays
for two inspector groups it does not use.

What moved
  - struct SCineGradeParams (Bypass Grade, Slope, Offset, Power, Saturation)
  - struct SCineLookParams  (LMT File)
Both keep their type GUIDs and their member ids, so the reflected shape of
each group is identical; only its owner changed.

What did NOT move
  - White Balance and Output (ODT) stay on the camera: they are what the
    camera is and what its monitor is. Note that the four
    Global_User_GradeWB* bus params are named "Grade" but are fed by the
    White Balance group - those writes are untouched.
  - The camera remains the ONLY writer of the Global_User_Grade* params and
    the only caller of CineCam::ApplyDisplayLuts. The renderer, the shaders,
    the bus parameter names and the LUT loading code are all unchanged.

The binding (decisions/s10-grade-component.md section 3, alternative P1,
appended there with the two rejected alternatives)
  ApplyGrade() and ApplyDisplayLuts() look the sibling up with
  GetEntity()->GetComponent<CCineGradeComponent>() on the frame they publish
  and fall back to file-static default-constructed neutrals when there is
  none. Chosen over a cached pointer / push handshake because it is immune
  to component creation order, a component removed at runtime falls back to
  neutral on the next frame with no dangling pointer, and the lookup sits
  inside the existing CanDriveRenderState() gate rather than adding a second
  path to the bus (f5891aab). Cost is a linear scan of a three-component
  entity, no allocation - the same call the plugin already makes each frame.
  The grade component adds one latency nudge: on its own
  ENTITY_EVENT_COMPONENT_PROPERTY_CHANGED (which the editor sends to the
  edited component only) it calls the camera's RefreshGrade(), which is the
  same two Apply calls behind the same gate. Without a camera sibling the
  component does nothing and logs one line, once.

Byte identity
  The neutral values are exact identities, and the bus' own registered
  defaults are already the neutral grade, so "no grade component" and "an
  untouched grade component" produce the same bus state RestoreGrade()
  writes - i.e. pure ACES 2.0, the same picture the camera's own Grade group
  gave at its defaults. Old saved levels lose the moved values (decided: no
  migration shim).

Docs: README.md gains a "The CineCam Grade component" section and the Grade
and Look tables move into it; docs/SceneReferredLook.md sections 1, 3 and 4
name the new component. Research note:
scene-notes/research/s10-grade-component-binding.md (outside git).

Build: CinematicCamera, Profile, 0 errors 0 warnings. Deployed to the LTS
and gamesdk trees.

